{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310525,"nonce":"1qTVlQ/siSZQY4X3k58YKtzNgjs=","title":"`{outer: inner_anon}` stored the inner anon's handle via `rayzor_anon_set_field_by_index`, but InsertFreePass treated all args of \\\"safe\\\" ...","message":"FIXED in `compiler/src/ir/insert_free.rs`. `rayzor_anon_set_field_by_index` was on the `anon_safe_ids` whitelist, which exempted ALL its args from escape analysis. But the THIRD arg (the stored value) does escape via the receiver — when the receiver later escapes (return / store / etc.), the value transitively escapes.\n\nAdded `anon_setter_ids` set tracking the setter functions specifically. In `pointer_escapes`, when a CallDirect targets a setter (∈ anon_setter_ids), the value arg (args[2]) is checked for escape even though the receiver/index args remain exempt.\n\nHow to apply: when introducing new anon accessors, decide per-arg whether each escapes via the receiver. Don't blanket-whitelist a function whose semantic is \"store value INTO receiver\" — the value half always escapes.\n\nSurfaced by: anon-of-anon literals like `{ inner: { x: 1 } }` returned from a method. Reading `outer.inner.x` on the caller side SIGSEGV'd. Confirmed by minimal repro `/tmp/nue_min.hx`. Not the same as [[bugs-anon-field-scramble]] which was a shape-mismatch bug — this one is escape analysis missing a heap pointer.\n\nCaveat: cross-function anon-of-anon STILL hits a separate bug when the variable receiving the result lacks a type annotation. `var loaded = ld.load(); var meta = loaded.metadata;` returns null fields; `var meta:Meta = loaded.metadata;` works. The inferred type at the binding site uses a different shape than the declared typedef. Workaround: explicit `:TypeName` annotation at the binding site. Long-term fix: TAST/HIR should propagate the typedef-target shape to var inference for cross-function anon returns.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_anon_setter_value_escape` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}